## 3.3 The Power Management System: Taming the Battery Drain

The single biggest obstacle when communicating with space is **energy**. Beaming a signal 500 kilometers into the atmosphere requires significantly more power than sending a signal 50 meters to a local cell tower. If the satellite radio ran at full power continuously, the battery would drain in under an hour.

To overcome this, the gateway uses an **Intelligent Sleep & Pulse Protocol** powered by **3GPP Release 18 low-power standards**.

```
                           POWER CONSUMPTION CYCLE
                           
   Active Space Beam
   (1.5W - 2.5W Peak)
     ┌─┐         ┌─┐                     ┌─┐
     │ │         │ │                     │ │
 ────┘ └─────────┘ └─────────────────────┘ └───────────────────
       ▲           ▲                     ▲
       │           │                     │
  [Text Sent]  [GPS Check]          [Ping Received]
  
  Idle State (Wi-Fi Only): ~150 mW
  Deep Sleep State:        < 5 mW

```

### The Three Power Modes:

1. **Active Mode (High Power - 1.5W to 2.5W):** The satellite modem turns on its radio amplifier only when a message or data packet needs to be sent up to space. This active window lasts for only a few seconds at a time.
2. **Wi-Fi Standby Mode (Medium Power - 150mW):** When no satellite transmission is happening, the satellite radio sleeps. The local Wi-Fi chip stays in a low-power listening state, ready to wake up the instant your phone sends a message.
3. **Deep Sleep / eDRX Mode (Ultra-Low Power - <5mW):** Utilizing **Extended Discontinuous Reception (eDRX)** from 3GPP Release 18, the device shuts down almost all internal circuitry during long periods of inactivity. It wakes up for a few milliseconds every couple of minutes to check if an incoming message is waiting in space.

